# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible: the deploy manifest is
# derived from it.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
TESSERA_DATABASE_URL=postgres://tessera:tessera@127.0.0.1:5432/tessera

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL and empty here: a development stack is one network. Set the first
# in the estate, where one pod answers for mainnet and testnet and the gateway
# stamps `CF-Network` on every request. See micro-deploy
# `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
TESSERA_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
#
# The network to assume when no `CF-Network` arrives. For `pnpm dev`, which has
# no gateway in front of it. NEVER set in production: it would let a request that
# lost its header be served from a network nobody named.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=


# Identity's JWKS and issuer. Token verification is no longer the only thing this service asks
# identity for — see IDENTITY_URL below — but it is still what these two are for. The credential
# worlds presents on POST /v1/provision is verified against this JWKS and must carry
# `tessera:provision` — an unauthenticated provisioning endpoint is a free-wards endpoint.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4100/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4100

# HMAC key for OUTBOUND event signatures, so a subscriber can prove an event came from us.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# The boot guard checks the SHAPE rather than a list of known-bad strings: base64 or hex only (no
# hyphens — every placeholder this estate ever wrote had one), at least 32 DECODED BYTES rather
# than 24 keystrokes, and a measured entropy floor. So a typed value is refused however long it is,
# and there is no NODE_ENV exemption to reach for (micro-org #142). Do not paste a placeholder back
# in: it will not boot, and that is the point.
OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# The same estate secret on the way IN. Deliveries this service receives are verified over the raw
# bytes before they are parsed (src/inbound.ts).
#
# A SEPARATE variable from OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET even though the deploy sets both to the same value
# today: the day the estate moves to per-producer signing secrets — which contracts-auth's
# `admin:audit:write` entry argues for at length — the two stop being one value, and a service that
# read one variable for both would have to be found first.
#
# A LIST, comma-separated, newest first. One key is a list of one, so a current deployment sets it
# exactly as it always has. It is a list because the estate's outbox secret is ONE key shared by 24
# services and one key cannot be rotated by swapping it: whichever end moves first has every
# delivery between them refused until the other catches up, and the failure does not announce
# itself — delivery partitions, and the symptom reads as a secret mismatch. So the rotation is a
# window. Add the new key at the FRONT, restart, move the producers, then remove the old one:
#   INBOUND_SIGNING_SECRET=<the new key>,<the old key>
# Every entry faces the SAME shape check as OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET above — base64 or hex, 32 decoded
# bytes, an entropy floor — and there is no weaker rule for the key on its way out of a rotation.
# The outgoing key is the one an attacker already holds if it leaked, and "just for the drain" is
# how a placeholder survives the rotation meant to remove it. Listing the same key twice is refused
# too, because a duplicate makes "which key verified this" ambiguous and that answer is how you
# know the rotation is finished.
INBOUND_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

# 4022. Argued at length in 23-tessera.md §10.1 and in src/env.ts: deliberately BELOW the derived
# 4100+ compose block, so no number of repositories appended to `deployableRepos()` can grow into
# it. emberkin binds 4100 (identity's compose host port), aetherholm 4120 (admin-api's) and nda
# 4110 (notify's) — three services whose own bind address is a number the estate hands to somebody
# else. CI fails this file if it disagrees with src/env.ts.
PORT=4022
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
TESSERA_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes — in both cases the thing an operator would search for
# after finding a stuck lease.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# Identity's ORIGIN, which is what POST /service-tokens/exchange is posted to. Defaults to
# IDENTITY_ISSUER, which is identity's origin on both estates — so the credential exchange arrives
# with no manifest change. Set this only on the day the issuer becomes a public URL and the
# in-cluster address stops matching it. An origin: no path, no query, no fragment.
# IDENTITY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4100

# --- optional upstreams; ABSENT IS A SUPPORTED MODE ---------------------------------------------
#
# Without STUDIO_URL and a credential, POST /v1/kiln/firings answers 503 `kiln_unconfigured` and
# every other route works. A world whose Kiln is cold is a world you can still walk around in, and
# a title that refused to boot over one optional dependency would be worse.
#
# STUDIO_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4111
# LEDGER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4102
# MARKET_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4113
#
# MARKET_URL and LEDGER_URL are BOTH required to activate a listing, or neither is used. Market's
# activation reserves the item in the ledger (`market/src/listings.ts`, holdEscrow with
# `kind: 'listing_item'`), so a market configured without a ledger would create a market draft,
# fail at the reserve, and leave a dead draft behind on every attempt. Without both,
# POST /v1/listings/:id/activate answers 503 `market_unconfigured` and drafting still works.
#
# micro-community, for ward governance. POST /v1/wards/:id/community answers 503 without it and
# every other route works — a ward with no community is the state every ward is minted in.
#
# It takes NO credential, deliberately. Community's create route refuses a service token outright
# ("accepting one would make every service in the estate a voting member of every community") and
# takes the owner from the caller's own token, so the only credential that works is the founding
# operator's, relayed per request. There is nothing here for this service to hold or rotate.
# COMMUNITY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4109
#
# --- the credential, and the variable it replaces (micro-org #222) -----------------------------
#
# THE LONG-LIVED, REVOCABLE CREDENTIAL this service EXCHANGES for a service token when it calls
# studio and the ledger. `cfsc_…`, minted by POST /service-credentials;
# deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh already writes one into tokens.env.
#
# It is exchanged at POST /service-tokens/exchange for an ordinary 600-second token, and
# @cloudsforge/auth's ServiceTokenProvider re-mints on traffic at a jittered 80% of each token's
# life — no timer, so nothing holds the event loop open past a drain, and a service with no
# outbound traffic mints nothing. The exchange consumes nothing, so N replicas boot from one
# credential and a restart days later still works.
#
# It needs `studio:write` (a service principal skips studio's ownership narrowing and names the
# acting user in the body, so a title generates on a player's behalf without impersonating them),
# `ledger:post` and `ledger:reserve` — see LEDGER_SCOPES in src/ledgerclient.ts, which is the
# declaration deploy/scripts/derive-grants.mjs actually reads.
#
# It does NOT need `market:write`, and that is the one line of this file worth reading twice.
# micro-market takes the seller from the token (`market/src/server.ts:681`, `subjectOf` at `:1486`)
# and has no on-behalf-of lane, so a listing created with this credential would have
# `service:tessera` as its seller — and market credits sale proceeds to its own sellerSubject
# (`market/src/orders.ts:388`). The creator would be paid nothing, silently. So both market calls
# relay the SELLER's own bearer token instead, and migration 11 CHECKs market's answer for the
# seller against Tessera's so the mistake cannot be stored even if that is one day forgotten.
# micro-community is the same shape for a different reason: its create route refuses a service
# token outright, so there is nothing here for this service to hold on that seam either.
# TESSERA_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=
#
# ── A MIGRATION AID WITH A STATED END. DELETE IT. ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# TESSERA_SERVICE_CREDENTIAL held a pre-minted TOKEN, read once at boot and handed straight to the
# studio and ledger clients — `token: async () => env.serviceCredential ?? ''`, src/index.ts:134
# and :172. A service token lives 600 seconds (identity/src/tokens.ts:33) and nothing could renew
# it. Measured on the live estate: the JWT in this variable had been expired for TWENTY-SIX HOURS
# on a container reporting healthy, because /livez verifies nothing and presents the credential to
# nobody. That is micro-org #222, and micro-org #197 is the same cliff across the estate.
#
# A LONGER EXPIRY IS NOT THE FIX. Rotation IS expiry: a 24-hour token is the same defect arriving a
# day later, hurting more, and useful to a thief for a day. The fix is the exchange above.
#
# The variable survives only so a container carrying the old value and not the new one keeps
# booting through a rolling deploy. It is now guarded like a credential, so the JWT that was
# actually in it is REFUSED BY NAME at boot — a deployment that has not migrated fails loudly at
# the door instead of quietly ten minutes later. While it is in use, src/upstreams.ts reports
# `mode: "static"` and src/index.ts logs `fatal` naming exactly what will break.
#
# DELETE THIS VARIABLE, and the `serviceCredential` field with it, once no estate sets it.
# TESSERA_SERVICE_CREDENTIAL=

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process (NODE_OPTIONS=--import @opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node/register), not by
# src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here, because this service never reads them.
#
# TESSERA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains "test"
# — the database-backed tests truncate every table this service owns.
